WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.040
Marvel's "Moon Knight" is here, with a&nbsp;
pair of personalities and a very cool suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.400
The first episode is full of twists&nbsp;
and turns, but it's the ending that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.880
reveals the most about Moon Knight&nbsp;
and his foes. Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.760
Steven Grant is a man tormented by a&nbsp;
situation that he doesn't fully understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.680
and he tries to control his tendency to&nbsp;
wander around while he's unconscious with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.920
a number of DIY tricks. It's clear that&nbsp;
this has been going on for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.360
and "Moon Knight" Episode 1 is&nbsp;
when everything finally changes.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.960
As the first episode nears its end,&nbsp;
Steven gets to peek behind the veil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.880
when he discovers physical evidence&nbsp;
of another personality's existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.520
tucked away in a hidey-hole in his apartment.&nbsp;
The cell phone he discovers is a particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.480
important find because it introduces viewers&nbsp;
to multiple potentially important characters.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.280
Shortly after discovering the old phone, Steven&nbsp;
gets a call. On the other end, an unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.600
woman named Layla has been trying to reach a&nbsp;
man named Marc for what appears to be weeks.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.680
"Who do you think I am?"

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.560
"What do you mean 'who'?&nbsp;
What's wrong with you, Marc?"

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.800
Layla might very well turn out&nbsp;
to be the MCU version of Marc's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.760
romantic interest in the comics, Marlene.
 
The other name in the missed calls list, DuChamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.080
is also very interesting. DuChamp is&nbsp;
almost certainly "Frenchie" DuChamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.120
Marc's old mercenary friend&nbsp;
and ally in the comics.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.680
It remains to be seen whether his name is a simple&nbsp;
Easter egg or a tease of the character's on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.120
debut. Still, the fact that he exists in the MCU&nbsp;
implies that Marc and Frenchie's old mercenary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.320
boss and Moon Knight's arguably greatest enemy,&nbsp;
Raoul Bushman also lurks somewhere in the world.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.640
While Marc's formidable nemesis seems like&nbsp;
the kind of character the MCU might want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.120
save for "Moon Knight" Season 2 or even&nbsp;
a movie, fans will no doubt welcome this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.200
low-key confirmation that Bushman might make&nbsp;
an appearance at some point down the line.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.760
In the comics, Moon Knight's various&nbsp;
personalities often converse with each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.800
and Khonshu is fond of making physical&nbsp;
appearances in all his bird skull-headed glory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.240
It's a pretty wild concept for a live-action show,&nbsp;
and indeed, "Moon Knight" episode 1 makes it clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.840
that the series intends to keep such interactions&nbsp;
at a relatively muted "voices in his head" level.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.520
During a mid-episode chase scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.960
F. Murray Abraham's Khonshu establishes himself&nbsp;
as a pestering presence that mostly uses his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.400
booming voice to express exasperation&nbsp;
over Steven's lack of mercenary prowess.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.840
"Ugh. The idiot's in control."

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.120
While the discovery of Marc's phone&nbsp;
finally clues Steven into the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.600
that he shares his body with another identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.120
it also spirals his life further into disarray.&nbsp;
His existence is suddenly all about Marc talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.320
to him through mirrors and Khonshu manifesting in&nbsp;
front of him in dramatic, horror movie fashion.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.600
Though these experiences are obviously no fun for&nbsp;
Steven, the mirror dialog is an effective visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.640
technique that translates the identities' comic&nbsp;
book conversations to the live-action medium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.200
Meanwhile, Khonshu's ominous, stalking hallway&nbsp;
appearance is at complete odds with the almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.760
flippant way the character's disembodied voice&nbsp;
converses with Steven earlier in the episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.200
This gives the viewer a glimpse of the&nbsp;
character's unpredictable, otherworldly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.840
nature, as well as the moon god's tendency to&nbsp;
ignore the mental well-being of its avatar.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.640
It becomes clear very quickly that Moon&nbsp;
Knight's primary nemesis, Arthur Harrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.200
is more than a small-time cult leader with a&nbsp;
penchant for filling his shoes with ground glass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.120
Not only does the villain have the powers&nbsp;
of Ammit, an Egyptian goddess of justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.120
but the cult he leads has also&nbsp;
infiltrated all levels of society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.320
This is revealed when Harrow confronts&nbsp;
Steven in the British Museum.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.360
"You really do work here. I'd&nbsp;
assumed that Steven Grant was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.920
an alias. Imagine my surprise to find you here."

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.880
Yet, the security guard, whom Steven knows and&nbsp;
who's clearly been at the museum for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.760
is a full member of Team Ammit. This fact is&nbsp;
confirmed when the guard reveals a scale tattoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.320
on his arm, similar to the one Harrow possesses.&nbsp;
It means that the guard has been at the museum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.120
for reasons that have nothing to do with Steven,&nbsp;
which in turn implies one of two things. Either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.760
Harrow placed the guard there for an entirely&nbsp;
unrelated purpose, or his cult is simply so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.640
formidable that he has followers everywhere. Considering the museum's vast Egyptology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.120
department with a vast collection of Ammit-themed&nbsp;
artifacts, the placement of the guard doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.360
seem accidental. And when Harrow proceeds to&nbsp;
outright state that his plans for the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.920
will cause millions of deaths, it becomes&nbsp;
clear that he's a global threat. While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.800
remains to be seen just how widespread the Cult&nbsp;
of Ammit is, this version of Arthur Harrow is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.840
definitely a far more powerful and dangerous&nbsp;
man than the mad doctor from the comics.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.640
As Harrow's summoned jackal creature corners&nbsp;
Steven in the museum bathroom and starts punching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.800
its way through the heavy doors, it's clear that&nbsp;
the beast's strength matches its supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.840
creepiness. Fortunately, a panicked mirror&nbsp;
conversation between Steven and Marc convinces the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.800
timid Brit to yield control of their shared body&nbsp;
just as the creature bursts through the doors.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:54.800
"Steven. Steven, I can save us. But I&nbsp;
can't have you fighting me this time."

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.280
What follows is yet another scene straight out&nbsp;
of a horror movie, at least for the jackal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.040
Moon Knight completely and utterly&nbsp;
overwhelms and destroys the beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.920
ignoring its feeble attempts to defend itself&nbsp;
and taking it down with a few brutal attacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.360
This short scene ends the&nbsp;
episode with a punch, literally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.960
And while it gives viewers the first good&nbsp;
look at the awesome Moon Knight costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.360
it also offers a sample of something even&nbsp;
more interesting, the character's powers.
 &nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.040
Moon Knight's strength is all over the&nbsp;
place in the comics, to put it mildly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.120
Sometimes, he's just a well-trained guy&nbsp;
with some gadgets and kevlar. Other times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.240
he commands a vast array of superpowers that allow&nbsp;
him to take down the Avengers all by himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.040
As such, it's interesting to see where on&nbsp;
this sliding scale the MCU character lands.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.960
Based on the fact that he effortlessly&nbsp;
defeats the superpowered jackal creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.040
it's clear that he's no weakling. There's no&nbsp;
telling what he can do when he unleashes his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.760
weapons and really lets loose, but we have&nbsp;
a sneaking suspicion that future episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.960
of "Moon Knight" will shed plenty&nbsp;
of light on his true power level.

